WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.635
FIPS 205 is the Stateless Hash-Based Digital Signature Standard.

00:00:03.635 --> 00:00:07.169
The standardized algorithm name is SLH-DSA, associated with SPHINCS+.

00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:12.275
At a high level, hash-based signatures use hash functions as their core foundation.

00:00:12.275 --> 00:00:17.773
That gives SLH-DSA a different design basis from lattice-based signature schemes such as ML-DSA,

00:00:17.773 --> 00:00:20.522
which can be valuable for algorithm diversity.

00:00:20.522 --> 00:00:25.235
SLH-DSA is not necessarily the default answer for every signature use case.

00:00:25.235 --> 00:00:28.376
It has tradeoffs involving signature size and performance.

00:00:28.376 --> 00:00:32.304
Larger signatures can affect bandwidth, storage, certificate chains, embedded devices,

00:00:32.304 --> 00:00:36.624
update packages, and protocols that were designed around smaller classical signatures.

00:00:36.624 --> 00:00:42.122
Slower signing or verification may matter in systems that process large volumes of signatures.

00:00:42.122 --> 00:00:44.871
Those tradeoffs do not make SLH-DSA unimportant.

00:00:44.871 --> 00:00:49.584
Conservative design and diversity can be valuable when organizations want a backup

00:00:49.584 --> 00:00:51.547
option based on different assumptions.

00:00:51.547 --> 00:00:57.045
Some use cases may tolerate larger signatures because the number of signatures is low,

00:00:57.045 --> 00:00:58.616
the artifacts are important,

00:00:58.616 --> 00:01:02.151
or the operational environment values a different cryptographic foundation.

00:01:02.151 --> 00:01:06.471
Security practitioners should understand SLH-DSA as part of the standards portfolio.

00:01:06.471 --> 00:01:08.435
It helps answer the question,

00:01:08.435 --> 00:01:14.325
what if we do not want every signature migration to depend on one mathematical family?

00:01:14.325 --> 00:01:18.645
The right choice still depends on system constraints, validation needs, performance,

00:01:18.645 --> 00:01:22.180
interoperability, and the risk model behind the signed object.
